    "math_academy_id": "q-81509",
    "knowledge_point_id": "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014400/assets/b8e6f6b7ab2081595723002737bcb73bbced52bfea0f8cfeb91b1201adab0160.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$",
    "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-2+9=7\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=7.$",
